# Chapter 18a: Installing Helpful Things

You only need **VS Code** and the **Malloy extension** to do everything in this textbook. This chapter lists other tools that make the work easier: converting data, hosting it online, and getting help from an AI assistant that knows Malloy. Install them as you need them, not all at once.

| Tool | What it's for | Needed? |
|---|---|---|
| [VS Code](#1-vs-code) | The editor where you write and run Malloy | ✅ Required |
| [Malloy extension](#2-the-malloy-extension) | Runs `.malloy` files and `.malloynb` notebooks, with DuckDB built in | ✅ Required |
| [Python + DuckDB](#3-python-and-duckdb) | Convert CSV files to Parquet ([Chapter 7](07_csv_and_parquet.malloynb)) | When you convert data |
| [Hugging Face tools](#4-hugging-face-tools) | Upload your own Parquet file to the internet ([Chapter 17](17_data_sources.malloynb)) | When you host data |
| [Git and GitHub](#5-git-and-github) | Store a project online, and run automatic jobs with GitHub Actions | For Chapter 22 |
| [Node.js and malloyyo](#6-node-js-and-malloyyo) | Check Malloy models, preview dashboards, and build dashboard websites | For Chapter 22 |
| [Claude Code or Antigravity](#7-an-ai-command-line-assistant-claude-code-or-antigravity) | An AI assistant that can read, write and run your code | For Chapter 22 |
| [Credible and Malloy skills](#8-credible-and-malloy-skills-for-ai-assistants) | Teach an AI assistant how to write good Malloy | Recommended with an AI assistant |

> 💻 **Opening a terminal.** Several steps say "in a terminal." In VS Code, choose **Terminal → New Terminal**. A terminal opens at the bottom of the window, already in the folder you have open. On a Mac you can also use the **Terminal** app; on Windows, **PowerShell**.

## 1. VS Code

1. Go to <https://code.visualstudio.com/> and download VS Code for your computer.
2. **Mac:** open the downloaded file and drag **Visual Studio Code** into **Applications**. **Windows:** run the installer and accept the defaults. Check **"Add to PATH"** if you see it.
3. Open VS Code.

## 2. The Malloy extension

1. In VS Code, click the **Extensions** icon in the left sidebar (four squares), or press `Ctrl+Shift+X` (`Cmd+Shift+X` on a Mac).
2. Search for **Malloy**.
3. Click **Install** on the extension published by **Malloy** (malloydata).
4. Choose **File → Open Folder…** and open your project folder, such as the `malloy-textbook-notebooks` folder.
5. Open `01_basics.malloynb` and click **Run All**. If you see results, you're set.

DuckDB is built into the extension, so there's no database to install. If something doesn't work, see [Chapter 18 · Troubleshooting](18_troubleshooting.malloynb).

## 3. Python and DuckDB

You need these to run the `csv_to_parquet.py` script from [Chapter 7](07_csv_and_parquet.malloynb).

1. **Install Python.**
   * **Mac:** download it from <https://www.python.org/downloads/>, or, if you use Homebrew, run `brew install python`.
   * **Windows:** download it from <https://www.python.org/downloads/>. On the first screen of the installer, check **"Add python.exe to PATH"**.
2. **Install the DuckDB Python package.** In a terminal:

   ```
   pip install duckdb
   ```

   On a Mac, if `pip` isn't found, use `pip3` instead. In the same way, use `python3` wherever this book says `python`.
3. **Check it.**

   ```
   python -c "import duckdb; print(duckdb.__version__)"
   ```

   If it prints a version number, it worked.

> 🦆 **Optional: the DuckDB command line.** DuckDB also has its own program for running SQL on files directly, which is handy for a quick look at a CSV. Mac: `brew install duckdb`. Windows: `winget install DuckDB.cli`. Then run `duckdb` in a terminal. See <https://duckdb.org/docs/installation/>.

## 4. Hugging Face tools

[Chapter 17](17_data_sources.malloynb#example-hosting-your-own-data-on-hugging-face) shows how to put your own Parquet file on Hugging Face, so Malloy can read it from a URL. You can upload files in the browser, but the command-line tools are easier for big files.

1. Create a free account at <https://huggingface.co/join>.
2. In a terminal:

   ```
   pip install -U huggingface_hub
   ```

3. Create an **access token**: on Hugging Face, go to **Settings → Access Tokens → Create new token**, choose **Write**, and copy it.
4. Log in, and paste the token when it asks:

   ```
   hf auth login
   ```

> 🔐 **Treat your token like a password.** Never paste it into a notebook, a `.malloy` file, or anything you upload or turn in.

## 5. Git and GitHub

[Chapter 22](22_homework_live_data_site.malloynb)'s project lives in a GitHub repository. You need a GitHub account and Git on your computer. The GitHub command line (`gh`) is optional, but it lets your AI assistant create the repository and turn on settings for you.

1. Create a free account at <https://github.com/signup>.
2. **Install Git.**
   * **Mac:** open Terminal and run `git --version`. If Git isn't installed, your Mac offers to install it (the "command line developer tools"). Click **Install**. Or, with Homebrew: `brew install git`.
   * **Windows:** download **Git for Windows** from <https://git-scm.com/download/win> and accept the defaults. It also gives you **Git Bash**, which some AI tools use.
3. **Install the GitHub CLI** (optional).
   * **Mac:** `brew install gh`
   * **Windows (PowerShell):** `winget install GitHub.cli`
4. Sign in: run `gh auth login`, choose **GitHub.com**, then **HTTPS**, then **Login with a web browser**.
5. Check it: `git --version` and `gh auth status`.

## 6. Node.js and malloyyo

**malloyyo** is a command-line tool for Malloy projects. It checks your model and dashboards (`malloyyo lint`), runs SQL with its own built-in DuckDB (`malloyyo sql`), previews a dashboard on your computer (`malloyyo dashboard dev`), and builds a static website from it (`malloyyo dashboard bundle`). It also gives an AI assistant a way to compile and run Malloy, so the AI can check its own work. [Chapter 22](22_homework_live_data_site.malloynb) shows how.

malloyyo needs **Node.js version 22 or newer**. Node.js comes with **npm**, which installs JavaScript programs, and **npx**, which runs them without installing (the skills installer in Section 8 uses it).

> ⚠️ **Use the right package name.** The old `malloyyo` package was removed from npm, so `npm install malloyyo` fails. The current package is **`@malloydata/malloyyo`**.

### Mac

1. Go to <https://nodejs.org>, download the **LTS** macOS Installer (`.pkg`), open it from your Downloads folder and click through. (With Homebrew, `brew install node` works too.)
2. Open a **new** Terminal window and check it worked:

   ```
   node -v
   npm -v
   ```

   `node -v` should print `v22` or higher.
3. Install malloyyo:

   ```
   npm install -g @malloydata/malloyyo
   ```

   If you get a permissions error, run it again with `sudo` in front (`sudo npm install -g @malloydata/malloyyo`) and type your Mac password.
4. Check it: `malloyyo --help`

### Windows

1. Install Node.js in one of two ways:
   * **Installer:** download the Windows Installer (`.msi`, **LTS**) from <https://nodejs.org> and click through with the default options.
   * **Command line:** open PowerShell and run `winget install OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS`.
2. Close PowerShell, open a **new** window, and check `node -v` and `npm -v`.
3. Install malloyyo:

   ```
   npm install -g @malloydata/malloyyo
   ```

4. Check it: `malloyyo --help`

If PowerShell says **"running scripts is disabled"** when you run `npm` or `malloyyo`, run this once and try again:

```
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned
```

### Updating

malloyyo changes often. To get the newest version, run the install command again: `npm install -g @malloydata/malloyyo@latest`.

## 7. An AI command-line assistant: Claude Code or Antigravity

An **AI CLI** is an AI assistant that works inside your project folder from a terminal. You describe what you want in plain English, such as *"write a query that shows sales by month"* or *"why does this cell give an error?"*, and it reads your files, writes code, and can run commands. Install **one** of these. Neither needs Node.js.

### Claude Code

Claude Code needs a Claude account (a paid plan or an API account). It runs on macOS 13 or newer and Windows 10 (version 1809) or newer.

* **Mac (Terminal):**

  ```
  curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh | bash
  ```

  Or, with Homebrew: `brew install --cask claude-code`
* **Windows (PowerShell):**

  ```
  irm https://claude.ai/install.ps1 | iex
  ```

* **Windows (Command Prompt):**

  ```
  curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.cmd -o install.cmd && install.cmd && del install.cmd
  ```

The install command **shows no progress** while it downloads, so be patient. When it finishes, open a **new** terminal window and run `claude --version`. A working install prints a version number. Then go to your project folder and run `claude` to sign in.

On Windows you don't need to run as Administrator. Installing **Git for Windows** (Section 5) is recommended, because it provides Git Bash, which some of Claude Code's tools use.

**Prefer a sidebar?** Claude Code also has a VS Code extension: in the **Extensions** panel, search for **Claude Code**, install the one published by **Anthropic**, then click the Claude icon in the sidebar and sign in. Full instructions: <https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup>.

### Google Antigravity CLI

The Antigravity CLI signs in with a Google account.

* **Mac (Terminal):**

  ```
  curl -fsSL https://antigravity.google/cli/install.sh | bash
  ```

  It installs to `~/.local/bin`. Run `source ~/.zshrc` (or open a new Terminal window) so the command is found. Or, with Homebrew: `brew install --cask antigravity-cli`
* **Windows (PowerShell):**

  ```
  irm https://antigravity.google/cli/install.ps1 | iex
  ```

* **Windows (Command Prompt):**

  ```
  curl -fsSL https://antigravity.google/cli/install.cmd -o install.cmd && install.cmd && del install.cmd
  ```

The command is **`agy`**. The first time you run it, it opens your web browser so you can sign in with your Google account.

> 🔁 **"Command not found"?** After installing anything, **close the terminal and open a new one**. A terminal only learns about new programs when it starts.

> 🧠 **Use it to learn, not to skip learning.** An AI assistant is great for explaining an error, suggesting a query, or checking your work. But you're graded on understanding your own work. Read what it writes, and be able to explain it. Follow your course's policy on AI use.

## 8. Credible and Malloy skills for AI assistants

A **skill** is a folder of instructions that teaches an AI assistant how to do a specific job well. The **Malloy and Credible skills** teach the assistant Malloy's rules and common mistakes: how to write joins and measures, how to build charts, how to avoid wrong totals, and so on. With the skills installed, the assistant writes much better Malloy.

Skills are just folders, each containing a `SKILL.md` file. The assistant finds them because they sit in a special folder **inside the project you're working in**:

```
my-project/                  ← the folder you open in VS Code
├── .claude/
│   └── skills/
│       ├── malloy-queries/
│       │   └── SKILL.md
│       ├── malloy-model/
│       │   └── SKILL.md
│       └── …
├── my_data.malloy
└── my_analysis.malloynb
```

There are **two ways** to get them.

### Way 1: the Credible VS Code extension

If you have a **Credible** account (your instructor will tell you if your class has one), the Credible extension installs the skills for you and keeps them up to date.

1. In the **Extensions** panel, search for **Credible** and install the extension published by **Credible**.
2. Click the Credible icon in the sidebar and **Sign In**.
3. Open your project folder (**File → Open Folder…**).
4. Open the Command Palette (`Ctrl+Shift+P`, or `Cmd+Shift+P` on a Mac), type **Credible: Enable Credible (Workspace)** and press Enter.

The extension then writes the skills into your project folder:

* `.claude/skills/`: the skills, for Claude Code
* `.agents/skills/` and `.cursor/rules/`: the same skills, for other AI tools such as Copilot and Cursor
* `.claude/rules/credible-modeling.md`: a rule telling Claude to read the skills whenever you ask about Malloy

These include the `credible-*` skills (publishing and documenting models on Credible) as well as the `malloy-*` skills. When a new version comes out, the extension updates them automatically. It also adds these folders to your `.gitignore`, so they aren't uploaded if you put your project on GitHub.

### Way 2: download the skills into your project folder

No Credible account? The `malloy-*` skills are **free and open source**, published by the Malloy team. Download them straight into the folder you're working in.

**Option A: one command (needs Node.js, Section 6).** In VS Code, open your project folder, open a terminal (**Terminal → New Terminal**), and run:

```
npx -y @malloy-publisher/skills@latest install claude
```

That copies every skill into `.claude/skills/` in the current folder. To see what you got:

```
npx -y @malloy-publisher/skills@latest list
```

Using a different AI tool, such as Antigravity? Run `npx -y @malloy-publisher/skills@latest install agents` instead. That puts the same skills in `.agents/skills/`, the folder many other AI tools read. If your tool doesn't pick them up on its own, tell it: *"Read the skills in .agents/skills before writing Malloy."*

Keep the `@latest`, so you always get the newest skills. To install them once for **every** project on your computer, add `--global` (they go into `.claude/skills/` in your home folder).

**Option B: download by hand (no Node needed).**

1. Go to <https://github.com/malloydata/publisher>.
2. Click the green **Code** button, then **Download ZIP**.
3. Unzip it, and open the `skills` folder inside. Each folder in it (`malloy-queries`, `malloy-model`, `malloy-charts`, …) is one skill.
4. In your project folder, make a folder named `.claude`, and inside it a folder named `skills`.
5. Copy the skill folders into `my-project/.claude/skills/`. Copy the **folders**, not just the `SKILL.md` files, because some skills have a `reference` folder too. You can skip the `eval-*` folders; they're for testing models, not for writing Malloy.

> 👀 **Can't see the `.claude` folder?** Names that start with a dot are hidden. **Mac Finder:** press `Cmd+Shift+.` to show them. **Windows File Explorer:** **View → Show → Hidden items**. VS Code's file list shows them either way.

### Check that the skills work

Open Claude Code in your project folder and ask:

> *What Malloy skills do you have?*

It should list skills such as `malloy-queries` and `malloy-model`. Then try a real request, such as *"Read my_data.malloy and suggest three measures I could add."*

> 💡 **Read a skill yourself.** The skills are written in plain English and make a good cheat sheet. You can read any of them online without installing anything, such as the [getting-started skill](https://unpkg.com/@malloy-publisher/skills@latest/skills/malloy-getting-started/SKILL.md) or the [queries skill](https://unpkg.com/@malloy-publisher/skills@latest/skills/malloy-queries/SKILL.md).

## Summary

* **Required:** VS Code and the Malloy extension. DuckDB is built in.
* **For converting data:** Python and `pip install duckdb`.
* **For hosting data:** a Hugging Face account and `pip install -U huggingface_hub`, then `hf auth login`.
* **For automatic data and dashboards (Chapter 22):** Git and a GitHub account, Node.js 22+ and `npm install -g @malloydata/malloyyo`.
* **For AI help:** Claude Code (`claude`) or the Antigravity CLI (`agy`), plus the Malloy skills in `.claude/skills/` inside your project folder.
* **"Command not found"?** Close the terminal and open a new one.
* **Two ways to get the skills:** the Credible VS Code extension (**Enable Credible (Workspace)**), or download them yourself with `npx -y @malloy-publisher/skills@latest install claude` or the ZIP from GitHub.
